.TH TUI_CP_WIDTH 3 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_cp_width, tui_cp_sanitize, tui_str_width, tui_strn_width, tui_str_fit,
tui_str_fit_ellipsis, tui_utf8_decode, tui_utf8_encode, tui_utf8_seq_len \-
UTF-8 and the display width of text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/text.h>
.PP
.BI "int      tui_cp_width(uint32_t " cp );
.BI "uint32_t tui_cp_sanitize(uint32_t " cp );
.BI "int      tui_str_width(const char *" s );
.BI "int      tui_strn_width(const char *" s ", size_t " n );
.BI "size_t   tui_str_fit(const char *" s ", int " cols ", int *" used );
.BI "int      tui_str_fit_ellipsis(const char *" s ", int " cols ", char *" out ", size_t " outsz );
.PP
.BI "int      tui_utf8_decode(const char *" s ", size_t " n ", uint32_t *" cp );
.BI "int      tui_utf8_encode(uint32_t " cp ", char " out [4]);
.BI "int      tui_utf8_seq_len(unsigned char " lead );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR tui_cp_width ()
returns the number of columns a terminal gives the code point
.IR cp :
0 for combining and zero-width characters, 2 for wide (East Asian wide
and full-width, most emoji), 1 for the rest, and \-1 for a control
character (C0, DEL, C1).
.PP
The widths come from a table built into the library, generated from
glibc's C.UTF-8 locale, so the program's locale makes no difference: in
the "C" locale glibc's own
.BR wcwidth (3)
reports \-1 for every non-ASCII character, and code relying on it drops
accented and CJK text.
A printable code point the table does not know is one column wide; text
is never silently dropped.
.PP
.BR tui_cp_sanitize ()
returns U+00B7 for a control character (C0, DEL, and C1 including the
8-bit CSI U+009B) and
.I cp
otherwise.
The drawing functions apply it to every character, so text from an
untrusted source cannot carry an escape sequence onto the screen.
.PP
.BR tui_str_width ()
and
.BR tui_strn_width ()
return the width of a UTF-8 string (or of its first
.I n
bytes) as drawn: a control counts as the one column of its replacement,
a malformed byte as one U+FFFD.
.PP
.BR tui_str_fit ()
returns how many bytes of
.I s
fit into
.I cols
columns without cutting a character; zero-width characters after the
last fitting one are included.
.I used
(may be NULL) receives their width.
.PP
.BR tui_str_fit_ellipsis ()
copies
.I s
into
.IR out ,
shortened to
.I cols
columns with a final "\[u2026]" (U+2026, one column) when it does not fit.
.PP
.BR tui_utf8_decode ()
decodes one code point from at most
.I n
bytes.
Malformed input \(em a bad lead byte, a missing continuation, an overlong
form, a surrogate, a value above U+10FFFF \(em yields U+FFFD and consumes
one byte; a sequence cut short by
.I n
yields U+FFFD and consumes the bytes present.
.BR tui_utf8_encode ()
encodes
.I cp
(an invalid one as U+FFFD), and
.BR tui_utf8_seq_len ()
gives the length a lead byte announces, 1 for an invalid lead.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR tui_str_fit_ellipsis ()
returns the width of the result, or \-1 if
.I outsz
is too small for it; then
.I out
holds the longest prefix that fits, never half a character.
.BR tui_utf8_decode ()
returns the bytes consumed, 0 for empty input;
.BR tui_utf8_encode ()
the bytes written, 1 to 4.
.SH NOTES
The table is regenerated at development time with the
.B regen-width
build target, and a unit test checks it against the running glibc.
It is not shared with any terminal emulator used to test the library.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_screen_put (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
